{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 12, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-397b-a17b", "content": "Raymond III served as the Count of Tripoli from the year 1152 until his death in 1187 and he played a pivotal role within the Kingdom of Jerusalem during the twelfth century. His life story involves complex political maneuvering alongside significant military engagements that shaped the fate of the Crusader states in the Levant.\n\n*Early Life and Succession*\nRaymond inherited his title after his father Raymond II suffered assassination at the hands of the Hashshashin order. This tragic event forced the young nobleman to assume leadership responsibilities while still quite youthful. He faced immediate challenges regarding the security of his county since external threats loomed large over the region. The Nur ad-Din forces presented constant danger so Raymond had to strengthen fortifications and seek alliances with neighboring Christian lords. His diplomatic skills became evident early on because he managed to maintain stability despite ongoing pressure from Muslim powers surrounding his territory.\n\n*Regency and Political Conflict*\nThe count later acted as regent for Baldwin IV who suffered from leprosy and could not rule without assistance. This period marked intense internal strife within the kingdom since various factions vied for power and influence over the young king. Raymond supported the noble party against the court party led by Guy of Lusignan and his supporters. Tensions escalated when Sibylla married Guy against the wishes of many barons including Raymond himself. The count attempted to prevent Guy from gaining the throne yet his efforts ultimately failed after Baldwin IV passed away. These political struggles weakened the kingdom internally just before a major external catastrophe occurred.\n\n*Battle of Hattin and Legacy*\nRaymond participated in the disastrous Battle of Hattin in 1187 where Saladin defeated the Crusader army decisively. 